{:snippets [{:description "Connect a project to an installed language server.", :tags #{:eglot :development :built-in :language-server}, :command "eglot", :title "Let Eglot do the introductions", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "eglot", :url "https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_mono/eglot.html", :code ";; Emacs 29+, with clangd installed separately.\n(use-package eglot\n  :ensure nil\n  :hook ((c-mode . eglot-ensure)\n         (c++-mode . eglot-ensure)))", :body ["Eglot is built into Emacs 29 and later. You still need to install a language server for your language, such as clangd for C and C++." "Open a source file and run M-x eglot. Emacs offers completion, diagnostics, and navigation using its ordinary editing commands." "Start interactively before adding the use-package declaration. That makes missing executables and project configuration problems easier to understand. The example starts Eglot in C and C++ buffers; adapt the hooks for your language." "Completion, project navigation, Flymake diagnostics, and Eldoc documentation use familiar built-in interfaces rather than a separate dashboard."]} {:description "See documentation for the thing at point.", :tags #{:eglot :development :documentation :built-in}, :tip? true, :command "eldoc-doc-buffer", :title "Documentation where you are", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "eldoc", :url "https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Programming-Language-Doc.html", :code "(use-package eldoc\n  :ensure nil\n  :init (global-eldoc-mode 1))", :body ["Eldoc shows contextual documentation in the echo area when the current mode provides it. Global Eldoc mode enables it in supported buffers, including Emacs Lisp." "When the echo area is too small, run M-x eldoc-doc-buffer to open a dedicated documentation buffer. This command is available in Emacs 28 and later." "Eglot supplies Eldoc information for its managed buffers. Enabling Eldoc alone does not install a language server or invent documentation for modes without a provider."]} {:description "See buffer diagnostics without leaving Emacs.", :tags #{:diagnostics :eglot :development :built-in}, :tip? true, :command "flymake-show-buffer-diagnostics", :title "Catch the small mistakes early", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "flymake", :url "https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_mono/flymake.html", :code "(use-package flymake\n  :ensure nil\n  :hook (emacs-lisp-mode . flymake-mode))", :body ["Flymake displays diagnostics supplied by the current mode or another backend. The example enables the built-in Emacs Lisp backend; it does not assume that a checker is available for every programming language." "Run M-x flymake-show-buffer-diagnostics for a list, or use flymake-goto-next-error and flymake-goto-prev-error to move between findings." "Eglot already enables Flymake in buffers it manages. You do not need a second hook for those buffers, and other languages may need an appropriate backend or external checker."]} {:description "Read minibuffer candidates in a vertical list.", :tags #{:built-in :minibuffer :completion}, :tip? true, :command "icomplete-vertical-mode", :title "Give completion a little room", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "icomplete-vertical", :url "https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Icomplete.html", :code "(use-package icomplete\n  :ensure nil\n  :config\n  (icomplete-mode 1)\n  (icomplete-vertical-mode 1))", :body ["Icomplete is a built-in minibuffer completion interface. Its vertical mode, available in Emacs 28 and later, puts candidates on separate lines instead of squeezing them into the prompt." "Try M-x after enabling these modes. The vertical list is also one of the inspirations for this site's command interface." "Use one completion display at a time. If you already use Vertico or another minibuffer completion frontend, disable it before trying Icomplete."]} {:description "Reopen a file at the position you last visited.", :tags #{:built-in :files}, :tip? true, :command "save-place-mode", :title "Pick up where you left off", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "keep-your-place", :url "https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Saving-Emacs-Sessions.html", :code "(use-package saveplace\n  :ensure nil\n  :init (save-place-mode 1))", :body ["Emacs can remember your position in files between sessions. Add this use-package declaration to your init file, or try M-x save-place-mode." "It is a small convenience that becomes especially useful when you move between long notes, source files, and documentation."]} {:description "Understand a change before you commit it.", :tags #{:version-control :git}, :command "magit-status", :title "Magit", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "magit", :url "https://magit.vc/", :code "(use-package magit\n  :ensure t\n  :bind (\"C-x g\" . magit-status))", :body ["Magit is a text-first interface to Git. Its status buffer brings the working tree, staged changes, and recent history together." "Start with M-x magit-status. Stage a hunk rather than a whole file, review the diff, and make a small, purposeful commit." "Magit is an optional package, not part of Emacs itself. Configure a package archive you trust before using :ensure t; the declaration installs it if needed and binds C-x g to its status buffer."]} {:description "Repeat related commands without repeating the whole key sequence.", :tags #{:navigation :built-in}, :tip? true, :command "repeat-mode", :title "Give a command an encore", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "repeat-mode", :url "https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Repeating.html", :code "(use-package repeat\n  :ensure nil\n  :init (repeat-mode 1))", :body ["Enable repeat-mode in Emacs 28 or later. Commands that provide a repeat map let you keep going with a single key." "Try C-x o to move to another window, then press o again. The echo area tells you which repeat keys are available. C-g leaves the repeat map."]} {:description "Keep minibuffer history between Emacs sessions.", :tags #{:history :built-in :minibuffer}, :tip? true, :command "savehist-mode", :title "Keep the minibuffer's memory", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "savehist", :url "https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Saving-Emacs-Sessions.html", :code "(use-package savehist\n  :ensure nil\n  :init (savehist-mode 1))", :body ["Savehist remembers minibuffer histories across sessions. Commands, search terms, and other entries are easier to recall with M-p instead of typing them again." "The library is named savehist, not save-hist. It is built in, so no package installation is needed." "History is written locally to savehist-file. Treat that file as personal data: do not commit it or add sensitive histories such as credentials."]} {:description "Pause after a prefix key to see what you can press next.", :tags #{:discovery :which-key :built-in :keybindings}, :tip? true, :command "which-key-mode", :title "Let the next key come to you", :author "hkjels", :language "elisp", :id "which-key", :url "https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Help.html", :code ";; Built into Emacs 30 and later.\n(use-package which-key\n  :ensure nil\n  :config (which-key-mode 1))", :body ["Which-key shows the available continuations when you pause after a prefix such as C-x or C-c. It makes unfamiliar bindings easier to discover without memorizing a wall of keys." "This example uses the version built into Emacs 30 and later. On an earlier Emacs, install which-key from a trusted package archive and change :ensure nil to :ensure t." "Try C-x and wait for the suggestions. C-g cancels the prefix; nothing is executed just by reading the list."]}], :constellation {:names ["tramp" "org" "magit" "dired" "eglot" "eshell" "consult" "vertico" "corfu" "embark" "calc" "avy" "orderless" "cape" "marginalia" "vterm" "info" "flymake" "abbrev" "ace-window" "aggressive-indent" "align" "ansi-color" "auctex" "a