cs itself. configure a package archive you trust before using :ensure t; the declaration installs it if needed and binds c-x g to its status buffer. version-control git"} {:path "/snippets/repeat-mode/", :title "Give a command an encore", :description "Repeat related commands without repeating the whole key sequence.", :type "snippet", :text "give a command an encore repeat related commands without repeating the whole key sequence. snippet give a command an encore  repeat related commands without repeating the whole key sequence.     by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / navigation enable repeat-mode in emacs 28 or later. commands that provide a repeat map let you keep going with a single key. try c-x o to move to another window, then press o again. the echo area tells you which repeat keys are available. c-g leaves the repeat map.  (use-package repeat\n  :ensure nil\n  :init (repeat-mode 1))  read the documentation     hkjels   repeat-mode (use-package repeat\n  :ensure nil\n  :init (repeat-mode 1)) enable repeat-mode in emacs 28 or later. commands that provide a repeat map let you keep going with a single key. try c-x o to move to another window, then press o again. the echo area tells you which repeat keys are available. c-g leaves the repeat map. navigation built-in"} {:path "/snippets/savehist/", :title "Keep the minibuffer's memory", :description "Keep minibuffer history between Emacs sessions.", :type "snippet", :text "keep the minibuffer's memory keep minibuffer history between emacs sessions. snippet keep the minibuffer's memory  keep minibuffer history between emacs sessions.     by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / history / minibuffer savehist remembers minibuffer histories across sessions. commands, search terms, and other entries are easier to recall with m-p instead of typing them again. the library is named savehist, not save-hist. it is built in, so no package installation is needed. history is written locally to savehist-file. treat that file as personal data: do not commit it or add sensitive histories such as credentials.  (use-package savehist\n  :ensure nil\n  :init (savehist-mode 1))  read the documentation     hkjels   savehist-mode (use-package savehist\n  :ensure nil\n  :init (savehist-mode 1)) savehist remembers minibuffer histories across sessions. commands, search terms, and other entries are easier to recall with m-p instead of typing them again. the library is named savehist, not save-hist. it is built in, so no package installation is needed. history is written locally to savehist-file. treat that file as personal data: do not commit it or add sensitive histories such as credentials. history built-in minibuffer"} {:path "/snippets/which-key/", :title "Let the next key come to you", :description "Pause after a prefix key to see what you can press next.", :type "snippet", :text "let the next key come to you pause after a prefix key to see what you can press next. snippet let the next key come to you  pause after a prefix key to see what you can press next.     by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / discovery / keybindings / which-key which-key shows the available continuations when you pause after a prefix such as c-x or c-c. it makes unfamiliar bindings easier to discover without memorizing a wall of keys. this example uses the version built into emacs 30 and later. on an earlier emacs, install which-key from a trusted package archive and change :ensure nil to :ensure t. try c-x and wait for the suggestions. c-g cancels the prefix; nothing is executed just by reading the list.  ;; built into emacs 30 and later.\n(use-package which-key\n  :ensure nil\n  :config (which-key-mode 1))  read the documentation     hkjels   which-key-mode ;; built into emacs 30 and later.\n(use-package which-key\n  :ensure nil\n  :config (which-key-mode 1)) which-key shows the available continuations when you pause after a prefix such as c-x or c-c. it makes unfamiliar bindings easier to discover without memorizing a wall of keys. this example uses the version built into emacs 30 and later. on an earlier emacs, install which-key from a trusted package archive and change :ensure nil to :ensure t. try c-x and wait for the suggestions. c-g cancels the prefix; nothing is executed just by reading the list. discovery which-key built-in keybindings"} {:path "/people/hkjels/", :title "Henrik Kjerringvåg", :description "Henrik Kjerringvåg", :type "person", :text "henrik kjerringvåg henrik kjerringvåg member record henrik kjerringvåg @hkjels  joined 2026-10 emacs since  2018  interests built-in / clojure / embedded / fp / org-mode  public github profile snippets  let eglot do the introductions  connect a project to an installed language server.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / development / eglot / language-server  documentation where you are  see documentation for the thing at point.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / development / documentation / eglot  catch the small mistakes early  see buffer diagnostics without leaving emacs.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / development / diagnostics / eglot  give completion a little room  read minibuffer candidates in a vertical list.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / completion / minibuffer  pick up where you left off  reopen a file at the position you last visited.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / files  magit  understand a change before you commit it.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  git / version-control  give a command an encore  repeat related commands without repeating the whole key sequence.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / navigation  keep the minibuffer's memory  keep minibuffer history between emacs sessions.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / history / minibuffer  let the next key come to you  pause after a prefix key to see what you can press next.  by   henrik kjerringvåg  built-in / discovery / keybindings / which-key back to people hkjels      org-mode clojure built-in embedded fp"}], :pages [{:id "about", :author "hkjels", :title "About the group", :description "An open door, not another tool to learn.", :body ["This is a place for people who use Emacs, used to use Emacs, or are curious about it. Bring a question, a small discovery, or a configuration that finally makes sense." "Short demos, practical notes, and conversation are more useful than perfect presentations. Beginners are welcome. You do not need a particular language, operating system, or level of experience." "This website borrows a few ideas from Emacs. A page is a buffer. The bottom bar is a mode-line. M-x is a quick way to find an action. You can also just use the links." "GitHub holds our published knowledge and membership records. Pull requests make changes; Discussions are for conversation. There is no separate account or private database here."]} {:id "scratch", :author "hkjels", :title "DNV Emacs User Group", :description "For people who use Emacs, used to use Emacs, or are curious about it.", :body ["Bring a question. Share a small discovery. Make yourself at home."]}], :meetings [], :members [{:github "hkjels", :full-name "Henrik Kjerringvåg", :joined "2026-10", :emacs-since "2018", :interests #{:org-mode :clojure :built-in :embedded :fp}}]}